Artemis the Ranger, cunningly disguised as wcristalle@hotmail.com wrote:

> To free up my hands a little, decided to put some tools I wasn't
> using into my bag of holding. Wasn't really paying attention. First
> one on the list was a bag of tricks...

You actually were lucky. The bag of tricks could have been the *last* one
on the list, and you'd have lost all other items you planned to put in the
bag of holding as well...

> I lost two (!!) dilithium crystals, a diamond, three rubies and other
> valuable gems, several potions of extra healing and enlightenment, a
> scroll of inventory, all my spare ammo, plus a lot of other stuff
> which I don't remember but I'm sure was valuable.

Well, the only things above I'd be sorry about were the potion of extra
healing (which by the way is useless if you're not carrying it in your
main directory), and the spare ammo (but that's easy to restock). What's a
scroll of inventory? Are you perhaps playing slash'em?

> Spent the next few minutes beating my head against the walls and
> repeating to myself, "It's only a game, calm down..."
> Learned one very important rule: do not put all your eggs in one
> basket. Or all your dilithium crystals in one bag. Even (or maybe
> especially!) if it's a bag of holding.

Wrong. You *should* be carrying only one bag of holding. Carrying more
than one only increases the risk of putting one inside the other.

What is much more dangerous is carrying any bag of tricks. I usually drop
these when and where I find them, or take them to the nearest altar and
empty them for sacrifices. When they're empty, you can safely put them in
your bag of holding. Wands of cancellation I also either drop immediately
or take them straight to my nearest stash.

> After reading the
> scroll, it will warn you and give the option to undo your move if you
> try to do something just plain stupid (i.e. moves which have a fatal
> or major deleterious effect. In other words, something which no player
> would ever really want to do in a million years.)

What about newbies that don't yet know what moves "have a fatal or major
deleterious effect"? If I'm playing for the first time, how would I know
that putting a BoT in a BoH would destroy things? Everyone should really
find things like this out themselves first. And since the game cannot know
who's playing, giving a warning would be no fun at all.

> You start to put the bag of tricks into your bag of holding. -More-
> Suddenly, you receive an urgent telepathic message from Mars: -More-
> "That action is highly unadvisable! Perhaps you should reconsider."
> Do you wish to proceed? (Yes/No)

NetHack is not, and should not be so forgiving. I sympathesize with people
that are killed by a gnome with a wand of death coming around the corner,
since there's hardly anything you can do about that, but getting a minor
disadvantage *because of your own stupidity* should not be so easily
prevented.

> Alternatively, it could be an intrinsic that you gain by wearing a
> ring/amulet, donating to priests/sacrificing at altars, wearing a
> special piece of armor (helm of foresight?)

Even worse than a scroll: a continuous item...

> I've always thought the ring of warning was pretty pathetic --
> perhaps it could be improved a little to warn of stupid actions.

Not all items should be swiss knives, with a hundred plus possible uses.
There should be good items, bad items, useful items, items with little
uses, ...

> So what do you think? A good idea?

Absolutely not.

